{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789314265,"nonce":"0wbrtZ3Y0v9bBO+IT03FJsprpWo=","title":"After a loop hands off to LLVM, the promoter still builds a second entry and a shared-module promote it never uses","message":"Timeline on bench_closure_call / bench_method_call (`ASH_OSR_LOG=1\nASH_TIER_LOG=1 ASH_PROFILE=all`): the Cranelift frame enters its OSR entry,\n`compile_with_llvm(main)` runs `publish_retier_entries` (module\n`retier_N`: AIR body ~2%, callees ~2%, `default\u003cO2\u003e` ~30%, MCJIT codegen\n~65%), the frame takes the exit within 0.2ms of `[retier] published`, and\nTHEN the promoter builds `osr_N_pc` -- the same module with the\ninterpreter-ABI prologue -- and promotes `main` through the shared module\n(11-19 bodies). For a loop owner called once (`main`) neither is ever\nentered: the frame is in LLVM code and no interpreter frame will reach the\nheader again. Measured on the Mac, that is ~1.7-1.8x the retier entry's\nown cost of promoter CPU per hot loop after the hand-off (the shared\npromote is ~25-33% of the `[tier] install ... in Xms` figure, the second\nentry the rest); on a 4-vCPU CI runner it overlaps the loop it no longer\nhelps. Whether that overlap is what widens CI's closure_call row (205ms\nhybrid vs 161 AOT, against 117 vs 117 on the NUC pinned to 4 real cores)\nis not established: inlined/free show the same extra CI penalty with no\nhand-off at all.\n\nTwo more costs inside each entry compile:\n- The OSR module carries 13-16 external-linkage bodies (the callee copies\n  and the native-caller thunks) that were all inlined into the entry but\n  cannot be dropped, so MCJIT codegens every one. Internal linkage for\n  everything but the entry lets `default\u003cO2\u003e` drop them (checked with opt:\n  13 defines -\u003e 1, entry unchanged); they are ~14-20% of the entry's\n  build-to-published time, the rest is the entry body and MCJIT's\n  per-module fixed cost.\n- `run_middle_end` builds a fresh TargetMachine per call (the AArch64\n  subtarget is rebuilt each time), ~10-15% of a small module's middle-end.\n\nOptions, in value order: skip the interpreter-ABI entry when a Cranelift\nentry is attached for the same header and the retier entry published;\nskip the shared promote for a function whose only activation is the frame\nthat just handed off (it still needs the ordinary body for future calls,\nwhich the own-module path now provides); internalize the OSR module's\nnon-entry bodies; cache the TargetMachine. O1 for the entry module is not\na lever (same steady state, ~2ms saved).","files":null}]}